WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
INTRO:
Daymond is likely the most conservative investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.840
on Shark Tank, but usually, it’s not because&nbsp;
he’s afraid of capital loss but because of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.280
investment philosophy. You see, all the sharks are&nbsp;
there for various reasons. Mark and Robert like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.080
invest in businesses that excite them. Kevin likes&nbsp;
to invest in businesses that carry low capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.680
risk. Lori likes to invest in ideas she can blow&nbsp;
up TV, and Barbara invests in entrepreneurs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.160
she believes in. But, Daymond doesn’t really&nbsp;
care about any of this. He’s not there to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.680
money or gain popularity. His primary goal is&nbsp;
to help entrepreneurs who really truly need it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.920
There are so many times that he’s turned down&nbsp;
super lucrative deals because he doesn’t feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.520
that the entrepreneur needs it. I think this makes&nbsp;
Daymond the most wholesome investor on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.120
and given his rough background, it makes&nbsp;
sense that he wants to help those in a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:07.200
situation. This wholesome investment strategy has&nbsp;
rewarded him quite handsomely given that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.880
the one to invest in Shark Tank’s most successful&nbsp;
business as of the end of 2021. So, here’s how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.120
Daymond went from the ghettos to Shark Tank and&nbsp;
became Shark Tanks’ most empathetic investor.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.680
LIFE IN THE SLUMS:&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.080
Taking a look back, Daymond was born&nbsp;
on February 23, 1969, in Brooklyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.120
New York. Initially, his life wasn’t that bad&nbsp;
financially given that he was an only child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.920
and his dad made enough money to make ends&nbsp;
meet. But, by the time Daymond was 10 years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.000
his parents would get a divorce, and Daymond&nbsp;
would be raised by a single mother. To make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.880
things worse, Daymond’s mother was a homemaker and&nbsp;
it was extremely difficult for her to find a job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.280
The family quickly sank into poverty and&nbsp;
ended up in the Hollis region of Queens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.200
This region is officially known as a low-income&nbsp;
African American neighborhood, but really, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.400
was a ghetto. So, to help out his mom through this&nbsp;
situation, Daymond began working odd jobs at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.760
age of 10. This included handing out fliers for&nbsp;
$2 an hour and becoming an electrician apprentice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.080
He also shoveled snow for neighbors and&nbsp;
sold pencils. Moving into high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.320
Daymond joined a program that allowed him to&nbsp;
attend school on an alternating weekly basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.800
This allowed him to hold a full-time job as a&nbsp;
waiter at Red Lobster, and run a small commuter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.840
van service on the side. Daymond says that this&nbsp;
program is what really ignited his entrepreneurial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.120
spirit. Anyway, moving on to college, Daymond&nbsp;
really had no intention of attending college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.120
First of all, he was dyslexic which made&nbsp;
traditional academics quite difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.080
and secondly, he didn’t have 4 years to go sit&nbsp;
in a classroom. He was already busting his butt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.320
to make ends meet, and quitting his job would&nbsp;
make it extremely difficult for his mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.160
So, he decided to not attend college and&nbsp;
continued on with his waiter job after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.960
high school. Daymond wasn’t satisfied with just&nbsp;
working this job for the rest of his life though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.160
He knew he wanted to create some sort of business,&nbsp;
but he didn’t know what exactly it would be about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.920
This would all change, however, when he&nbsp;
came across some overpriced wool ski hats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.320
on the market. Daymond was convinced he could&nbsp;
produce the hats for significantly cheaper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.200
so he bought some low-cost fabrics, learned how to&nbsp;
sew from his mother, and began knitting wool hats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.160
He was able to produce 80 hats, and he went around&nbsp;
the neighborhood and sold them for $10 apiece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.800
He made a solid $800 in revenue from the venture,&nbsp;
but for Daymond, selling these hats meant much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.680
more than just making money. You see, during&nbsp;
his teenage years, Daymond was a massive fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.560
of hip-hop and rap. He aspired to be like his&nbsp;
favorite artists, but he usually couldn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.240
afford the clothes they wore. By selling&nbsp;
affordable clothes rooted in hip hop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.640
Daymond felt that he could appeal to his younger&nbsp;
self and give modern kids what he never had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:38.800
And with that, Daymond founded For Us By Us&nbsp;
or FUBU in 1992. Daymond barely had any money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.200
himself, but his mother would pull through&nbsp;
and risk it all for Daymond. She mortgaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.080
her house and lent him $100,000 to start up the&nbsp;
business. The stakes couldn’t have been higher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.120
but this was Daymond’s one shot at a better life,&nbsp;
and there was no way he was going to give that up.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:56.880
FUBU:&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.440
From the very beginning, Daymond knew that the key&nbsp;
to growing a large clothing brand was exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.840
but he decided to start small and keep things&nbsp;
simple. Daymond continued to produce the wool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.120
hats that had already sold well, and he continued&nbsp;
working as a waiter. In the meantime, he started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.520
experimenting with sewing screen-printed&nbsp;
T-shirts. As the hat business grew, Daymond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.120
hired two of his childhood friends to start&nbsp;
selling the T-shirts around the neighborhood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.600
This worked out decently well at the beginning,&nbsp;
but selling clothes door to door clearly wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:29.120
a very good sales channel long term. So, Daymond&nbsp;
started leveraging consignments. A consignment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.720
simply an agreement with a store or vendor&nbsp;
to sell your products for a set commission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.600
or fee. Daymond was able to score a couple of&nbsp;
consignment deals with vendors in the Northeast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.920
and with that Daymond could start focusing&nbsp;
on actually building a brand. Daymond and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.800
friends started sewing the FUBU logo into hockey&nbsp;
jerseys, sweatshirts, and T-shirts, and they lent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.000
out this clothing to up-and-coming rappers to use&nbsp;
in their music videos. Within 2 years, Daymond was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.880
able to secure product placement within 30 music&nbsp;
videos which gave FUBU quite a bit of exposure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.720
People watching these music videos thought&nbsp;
that FUBU was a decently sized mall brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.680
but really, it was just Daymond a couple of his&nbsp;
friends. And this became painfully apparent when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.120
FUBU started to receive their first big orders.&nbsp;
In the early 1990s, Daymond traveled to Las Vegas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.240
to showcase FUBU at a fashion trade show called&nbsp;
MAGIC. This was definitely a worthwhile trip given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.840
that he received $300,000 worth of orders, and an&nbsp;
offer to be sold at select Macy’s outlets. Daymond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.080
happily took the orders, but only he knew that it&nbsp;
was going to be a massive struggle to produce that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.280
many clothes. As soon as he returned home, Daymond&nbsp;
applied for loans at every bank he could think of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.800
but he was rejected by all 27 banks. So, in&nbsp;
a last-ditch effort, Daymond use the last of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.040
his money to put out an advertisement in the New&nbsp;
York times looking for a manufacturing partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.680
And luckily for Daymond, Samsung Textiles&nbsp;
responded to the ad and helped FUBU complete their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.960
first mega-order. Around the same time, Daymond’s&nbsp;
hip-hop partners including Will Smith, Beyonce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:56.400
and LL Cool J were starting to get some notoriety.&nbsp;
LL Cool J and Daymond were actually childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.200
friends, and Daymond was able to convince&nbsp;
Cool J to wear a FUBU hat in a GAP commercial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.920
Cool J didn’t just stop right there either. He was&nbsp;
hired to rap in the commercial and he snuck in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:16.480
phrase For Us By Us in the rap. Honestly,&nbsp;
I’m not sure how GAP let this one slip by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:21.280
but it worked out great for FUBU. As their hip-hop&nbsp;
partners evolved into full-fledged celebrities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.720
FUBU transitioned from being a small&nbsp;
home business to a national brand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:33.840
In 1998, FUBU pulled in $350 million in&nbsp;
sales, but Daymond was just getting started.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.560
BUILDING AN EMPIRE:
While FUBU was doing better than ever before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.760
Daymond knew that hip-hop fame was fickle and that&nbsp;
it could disappear just as fast as it appeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:48.000
So, Daymond cleverly started to diversify as soon&nbsp;
as possible. In the late 1990s and early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.400
FUBU put hip-hop promotions on the back burner and&nbsp;
started to reinvest their profits into building an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:58.000
international audience. They primarily targeted&nbsp;
Saudi Arabia, China, Korea, Japan, and South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.360
Africa. Daymond didn’t just limit the company to&nbsp;
streetwear clothing either. In 2000, he launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.560
FUBU Entertainment also known as FUBU Records. As&nbsp;
the name suggests, this subsidiary was focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.520
producing and releasing music albums. One of their&nbsp;
first deals was with Universal Records which ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.720
up generating millions in revenue. Moving forward,&nbsp;
in 2002, Daymond embraced the internet with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:23.680
launch of y2g.com which sold their various&nbsp;
products online. All of these efforts gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.000
FUBU a pretty solid safety net, but Daymond&nbsp;
didn’t just want to rely on FUBU either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.280
so he also started ventures outside FUBU. For&nbsp;
example, Daymond leveraged his newfound fame to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.560
start selling books. So far, Daymond has published&nbsp;
4 books called Display of Power, The Brand Within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.040
The Power of Broke, and Rise and Grind. These&nbsp;
publications have won him the titles of New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.920
York Times and Wall Street Journal best-selling&nbsp;
author. Aside from publishing books, Daymond has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.520
also become a public speaker and he charges $50&nbsp;
to $75,000 per speech. Daymond has also partnered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.560
with several brands including Pitbull, Miss&nbsp;
Universe, Shopify, and most recently, Audible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.960
It’s a good thing Daymond diversified so heavily&nbsp;
as FUBU’s sales did indeed start dropping just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:09.440
like he expected. From their peak of $350 million&nbsp;
in 1998, FUBU declined to $200 million by 1999&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:14.000
and they’ve stabilized around there ever since.&nbsp;
Daymond doesn’t seem to mind too much though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.320
He knew the drop was coming and he prepared&nbsp;
as much as he could. Also, he had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:25.840
made more money than he ever wanted, and now,&nbsp;
he wanted to focus his efforts on giving back.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.320
DAYMOND’S PHILOSOPHY:
Daymond isn’t interested in making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.200
rich richer or even the middle class richer. His&nbsp;
primary goal is to help the poor break out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.320
poverty cycle. He knows firsthand how impossible&nbsp;
it was to secure a loan, and with Shark Tank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.200
Daymond has prioritized giving opportunity just&nbsp;
like Samsung Textiles gave him an opportunity. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.480
of the best examples of this was with Mo’s Bows.&nbsp;
In Season 5 of Shark Tank, a young entrepreneur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.880
named Moziah pitched his bowtie-making business&nbsp;
to the Sharks. He asked for a modest $50,000 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.960
a 20% stake in his business, but instead of&nbsp;
offering him the money, Daymond offered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.680
mentor him for free. Since then, Daymond has&nbsp;
helped Moziah secure a manufacturing partner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.040
get into Neiman Marcus, and even get a 7 figure&nbsp;
licensing deal with the NBA. Daymond literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.240
made this kid into a millionaire for free. I don’t&nbsp;
think it gets more wholesome than that. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:17.120
wholesome Daymond deal was with Bombas. Bombas is&nbsp;
a sock company that focuses on donating socks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.920
homeless shelters. The founders, David and Randy,&nbsp;
started the company after finding out that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.720
most needed item at homeless shelters was socks.&nbsp;
And for every item you purchase from Bombas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.360
they donate an item to homeless organizations. It&nbsp;
looks like this message has really resonated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.000
customers as Bombas is not only Daymond’s most&nbsp;
successful investment but the most successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.280
investment in Shark Tank history grossing a total&nbsp;
of $225 million in lifetime sales. Shark Tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.960
isn’t the only way Daymond gives back either. In&nbsp;
2015, he co-founded Daymond John’s Success Formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.360
which teaches entrepreneurs how to start and&nbsp;
grow their business. And that same year, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.240
appointed by President Obama to be an ambassador&nbsp;
for underserved entrepreneurs. Considering all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:03.840
this, I don’t think that there’s any doubt that&nbsp;
Daymond is the most selfless shark on the show.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.360
DAYMOND TODAY:
As for Daymond today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.960
he’s doing better than ever before. FUBU&nbsp;
crossed $6 billion in annual sales back in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:14.240
and they’re likely in the $7 to $8&nbsp;
billion range today if not even higher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:18.800
In terms of Shark Tank investments, Daymond&nbsp;
has completed 61 deals on air which comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.760
out to a total of $8.5 million invested.&nbsp;
Considering this, I don’t think you’d be too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.560
surprised to hear that his net worth is estimated&nbsp;
to be around $350 million. Unfortunately, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.560
his personal life isn’t progressing nearly as&nbsp;
smoothly. In April of 2017, Daymond was diagnosed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.680
with stage 2 thyroid cancer. He did undergo&nbsp;
surgery and get the cancerous nodule removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.640
but it’s definitely not something to take lightly.&nbsp;
Hopefully, the cancer never returns though as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.520
Daymond is not only relatively young at 52, but&nbsp;
he’s a phenomenal community-oriented person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.000
and he would be a massive loss for everybody.&nbsp;
Do you guys think Daymond is the best shark?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if&nbsp;
you think that the other sharks should take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.560
a couple of notes from Daymond. And of course,&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.000
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
